            <ArticleTitle>Enlarged coronary artery pseudoaneurysm after drug-eluting stent implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>608-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0218492312461454</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 73-year-old man with 2-vessel coronary artery disease underwent a staged percutaneous coronary intervention that resulted in rupture of the right coronary artery and pseudoaneurysm formation. Although the pseudoaneurysm regressed over a week, it reexpanded after a year. Resection of the pseudoaneurysm and coronary artery bypass grafting were performed. The drug-eluting stent at the coronary artery injury site may have delayed healing and remodeling of the artery, thus contributing to reexpansion of the pseudoaneurysm.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
